# 01: Movie list and day picker

**What to build:** A customer opens `/`, picks a day with the day picker (five day buttons and a datepicker), and sees the movies playing that day with their screening times, loaded from the backend, with loading, error and empty states. The selected day lives in the URL. Clicking a screening opens its seat map route. Also sets up the five routes from section 3 (screens 2–5 may be empty placeholders for now) and the redirect of unknown URLs to `/`.

**Specification:** sections 1, 2, 3 (routes), 3.3, 4, 9.1.

**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately).

**Status:** done

- [x] AC-01 — Without a chosen date, `/` shows today's screenings: one movie card per movie with a screening today.
- [x] AC-02 — Each card shows title, genre, runtime in minutes and format (`PL dubbing` / `PL subtitles`).
- [x] AC-03 — Each card renders one button per screening of that movie on the selected day, labelled with the screening time (`HH:mm`), ordered by time.
- [x] AC-04 — Clicking a screening button navigates to `/screenings/:id/seats` for that screening id.
- [x] AC-49 — While the movie request is in flight, `/` shows a loading indicator and no movie cards.
- [x] AC-50 — When the movie request fails, `/` shows `Failed to load movies` and a `Retry` button that re-issues the request.
- [x] AC-52 — Above the movie list, the day picker shows `Today` and the next four days by English weekday name, followed by a datepicker; `Today` is selected by default.
- [x] AC-53 — Choosing another day shows only the movies with a screening on that day, each with only that day's screenings.
- [x] AC-54 — A day without screenings shows `No screenings on this day` and no movie cards.
- [x] AC-55 — The selected day is kept in the URL as `?date=YYYY-MM-DD`; opening such a URL selects that day, and a missing, invalid or past date selects today.
- [x] AC-51 — Every movie-list identifier in section 9.1 is present with the exact names and values given there.
----
## 4. Movie list (`/`)

Page layout, top to bottom: the day picker, then the movie list.

### 4.1 Day picker

- Five day buttons: `Today`, then the next four days labelled with their English weekday in
  full. If today is Tuesday: `Today`, `Wednesday`, `Thursday`, `Friday`, `Saturday`.
- After them, a datepicker for choosing any other date. Dates before today cannot be
  chosen; there is no upper limit.
- Exactly one day is selected at a time. When the selected day is one of the five, its
  button is shown as selected; when it was chosen in the datepicker and is not one of the
  five, no button is selected and the datepicker shows the date.
- The selected day is part of the URL: `/?date=<ISO date>`. Choosing a day updates the URL.
  `/` without `date`, with a date that is not a valid ISO date, or with a date before today
  selects today.

### 4.2 Movies

Movies and screenings are loaded from the backend each time the screen opens.

- **Loading** — while the requests are in flight the screen shows the text `Loading…` and
  no movie cards.
- **Error** — if a request fails, the screen shows the text `Failed to load movies` and a
  `Retry` button. `Retry` issues the requests again (showing the loading indicator).
- **Loaded** — a vertical list of movie cards: one per movie that has at least one
  screening on the selected day, in the order the backend returns the movies. Each card
  shows:
  - the title,
  - the genre,
  - the runtime as `<runtimeMinutes> min`, e.g. `128 min`,
  - the format, exactly as in the data: `PL dubbing` or `PL subtitles`,
  - one button per screening of that movie on the selected day, labelled with its time,
    e.g. `16:00`, ordered by time.
- **Empty** — when no movie has a screening on the selected day, the screen shows the text
  `No screenings on this day` and no movie cards.

Clicking a screening button opens `/screenings/:id/seats` for that screening.

---

## 2. Backend

The backend is [`json-server`](https://github.com/typicode/json-server/tree/v0) 0.17.4
serving `db.json` from the workspace root. Start it with `pnpm api` and keep it running
while you work on the application.

- **Base URL:** `http://localhost:3000`
- CORS is enabled; the application calls the base URL directly.
- Every response is JSON. Requests with a body send `Content-Type: application/json`.
- `json-server` writes every successful `POST` into `db.json`. That is expected.

### 2.2 Endpoints

| Method | Path | Returns |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/movies` | `Movie[]` — 5 movies |
| `GET` | `/movies/:id` | `Movie`, or `404` |
| `GET` | `/screenings` | `Screening[]` — all screenings, in no guaranteed order |
| `GET` | `/screenings?date=:date` | `Screening[]` of one day, e.g. `?date=2026-09-15` |
| `GET` | `/screenings?movieId=:movieId` | `Screening[]` of one movie |
| `GET` | `/screenings/:id` | `Screening`, or `404` |
| `POST` | `/reservations` | `201` with the created `Reservation` |

`GET /screenings/1` returns:

```json
{
  "id": 1,
  "movieId": 1,
  "date": "2026-09-15",
  "time": "16:00",
  "occupiedSeats": ["R3C7", "R3C8", "R3C9", "R6C12", "R6C13", "R11C21", "R11C22", "R15C5", "R15C6"]
}
```

`POST /reservations` request body:

```json
{
  "screeningId": 1,
  "seats": ["R7C12", "R15C1", "R15C2"],
  "customer": {
    "firstName": "Anna",
    "lastName": "Kowalska",
    "email": "anna.kowalska@example.com",
    "phone": "123456789"
  },
  "totalPrice": 121
}
```

The response is the same object with an `id` added. Any network error or any status other
than `2xx` is a failed request. Creating a reservation does not change the screening's
`occupiedSeats`; the application makes no other write requests.

**Dates.** The screenings in `db.json` are between 15 and 21 September 2026. The
application always works from the browser's current date, so on other dates the movie list
may show `No screenings on this day` for every day it offers — that is expected. Do not
change `db.json`, and do not change the date logic to see screenings.

